.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt NEWPORT 4 sgimips
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm newport
.Nd SGI NG1 graphics controller
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "newport* at gio? slot ?"
.Cd "wsdisplay* at newport? console ?"
.Cd "options NEWPORT_DECOR"
.Cd "options NEWPORT_FRAME"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver supports the SGI NG1 (a.k.a. Indy 8-bit, Indy 24-bit, and
XL) graphics controllers, often found on Indy and Indigo2 machines.
.Pp
The experimental
.Dv NEWPORT_DECOR
kernel option enables a built-in vertical gradient behind live console text,
from black at the top to an Octane-style teal green at the bottom.
The driver reserves unused palette entries for the gradient while preserving
the terminal colours and their inverted cursor colours.
Black text backgrounds reveal the decoration; other background colours
remain opaque.
Scrolling redraws characters at their new positions to keep the decoration
fixed on the display.
This costs more rendering work than the normal accelerated scrolling.
.Pp
Decoration starts when the virtual console attaches.
Earlier boot messages use the normal solid background and are replayed
when the decorated console becomes available.
The option applies to all virtual screens on Newport displays, including
restoring text mode after a graphics application exits.
It does not change the text area or provide image loading or runtime
configuration.
.Pp
The
.Pa GENERIC32_IP2x
kernel configuration and its
.Pa GENERIC64_IP2x
and
.Pa GENERIC64_32_IP2x
variants enable this option and select white kernel messages for contrast
against the green background.
The
.Pa NEWPORT_DECOR
configuration inherits these defaults.
To restore a solid background in a configuration that includes one of these
files, add
.Dq no options NEWPORT_DECOR .
.Pp
The experimental
.Dv NEWPORT_FRAME
option selects an embedded image with a bevelled frame and grey text panel.
It implies
.Dv NEWPORT_DECOR
and is enabled by the
.Pa NEWPORT_FRAME
example configuration.
At 1280x1024 or 1282x1024, kernel messages and subsequent console output
occupy a 704x672 pixel rectangle inside the frame, starting at (288,176).
With the normal 8x16 font this gives 88 columns and 42 rows.
The same geometry applies to the early kernel console.
Scrolling and coloured clears preserve the surrounding image and padding;
virtual-screen and graphics-mode restoration repaint the frame.
Other resolutions use the full-screen gradient.
This option is not enabled in the generic kernels.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sgimips/gio 4 ,
.Xr sgimips/grtwo 4 ,
.Xr sgimips/light 4 ,
.Xr wscons 4
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Nx 2.0 .
